    "\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "cb8e5ff8",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Introduction To CCD\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "e0f4e16e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "A Charge Coupled Device is an integrated circuit containing an array of light sensitive elements called pixels which are made up of charge storing elements called capacitors, \n",
    "which under the control of an external circuit transfers its electric charge to neighbouring capacitor. When the light falls on the CCD photons are absorbed by the semiconductor material which generates electric charge. The amount of charge accumulated in each pixel depends on the intensity of the incident light and therefore brighter region produce more change while darker regions produce less. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "006611bb",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The operation of a CCD begins when incident photons fall on the photosensitive pixels. When a photon with sufficient energy is absorbed by the semiconductor, it transfers its energy to an electron, exciting the electron from the valence band to the conduction band. This process produces an electron hole pair. The generated electrons are then collected and stored within the potential well of the corresponding pixel. During the exposure, more photons produce more electron hole pairs, causing the charge stored in each pixel to increase with the intensity of the incident light."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "0c728f4b",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The generated electrons are stored in potential wells created by electrodes or gates beneath each pixel. By applying clock voltages, these potential wells are shifted from one pixel to the next, allowing the accumulated charge to be transferred systematically toward the readout region."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "97a73cef",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "After the exposure is complete, the stored charge is transferred through shift registers it controls the movement of charge from one pixel to the neighbouring pixel and eventually towards the readout region. The charge from each pixel is then delivered to a charge amplifier, where the amount of stored charge is converted into a measurable electrical voltage. Since the voltage is proportional to the amount of charge collected, it represents the intensity of light recorded by that pixel."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "7d632862",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Finally, the analog voltage signal produced by the charge amplifier is sent to an analog to digital converter (ADC). The ADC converts the continuous voltage into a numerical digital value, commonly represented as an Digital Number. These digital values are stored as the pixel values of the resulting image."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "491456c1",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Quantum Efficiency"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "1d53ac53",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Quantum Efficicency is the term used to represent the ability of a detector to turn incoming photons into useful output.It is defined as the ratio of incoming photons to those actually detected and stored in the device.\n",
    "For example, if there are 100 photons reaching a detector and 80 of them are useful and provide information about the image, the quantum efficiency will be 80%. This also means that 80% of every incident photon produces a detected electron. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "aea53a6c",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "For JWST, quantum efficiency tells us how efficiently incident infrared photons are converted into a measureable electronic signal in the detector. Quantum Efficiency is temperature sensitive and depends on the wavelength of the incoming photons."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "fcfc7093",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Flat Fielding"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "20485c0a",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Consider an image of a perfectly uniform light source taken through the telescope. Every part of uniform light source is eually bright. Every pixel on the detector should detect exactly the sane value. But real detectors are not perfectly uniform, different pixels can have different sensitivities. Some pixels may respond more strongly than others even when they receive the same amount of light. If these variations are not corrected then there might be lapse in accuracy astronomical image produced. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "663946ac",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Flat fielding is the process that is necessary to correct these variations in the detector response. A flat field image is obtained by illuminating the detector with a source that is as uniform as possible. The process consists of steps mentioned below"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "22b5228e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Point telescope at uniform light source, there are three ways of how this is done\n",
    "i) twilight sky\n",
    "ii) white panel inside dome\n",
    "iii) special lamp\n",
    "\n",
    "• This is raw flat field frame.\n",
    "\n",
    "• Take a bias frame -image with zero exposure, shutter closed,capturing electronic offset.\n",
    "\n",
    "• Subtract this from flat field.\n",
    "\n",
    "• Take many flat field frames to average out & remove noise (random).\n",
    "\n",
    "• When average them and you are left with any true underlying pattern of pixel imperfections.\n",
    "\n",
    "• Now normalize it and it rescales everything so that perfect every pixel in the flat field has an avg value of 1.\n",
    "\n",
    "• So normalized flat field is a relative pixel response with no units.\n",
    "\n",
    "• Take the actual science image and divide it pixel by pixel by the normalized flat field image.\n",
    "\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "d417e8ea",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Electron into Fluxes"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "1c1ab818",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "\n",
    "The detector records the incident light as a number of electrons. Dividing the total electron count by the exposure time gives the electron count rate, expressed in electrons per second. In detector readout, the measured signal is converted into ADU (Analog-to-Digital Units), with the relationship between ADU and the number of electrons determined by the detector gain. For JWST, calibrated Level 2 and Level 3 data products are commonly expressed in units of MJy/sr (mega janskys per steradian), where the steradian represents the solid angle on the sky. The calibrated flux can then be converted into AB magnitude using the relation\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "d7ba5d63",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "$$\n",
    "m_{\\rm AB} = -2.5\\log_{10}(F) + \\text{constant}\n",
    "$$"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "e48d578c",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "$$\n",
    "\\Delta m = -2.5\\log_{10}\\left(\\frac{F_1}{F_2}\\right)\n",
    "$$"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "2bdb63f1",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "\n",
    "The magnitude scale is inverse to brightness, meaning that brighter sources have smaller magnitude values. Vega is traditionally used as a reference point in the Vega magnitude system, with its magnitude defined to be approximately zero in the relevant bands.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "81c5867f",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Point Spread Function(PSF)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "d5dbb9da",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The PSF describes how a telescope and the detector records the light from an ideal point source of light, such as a distant star. But due to the effect of intrument, diffraction and other optical effects there is bluring of the point source and spreading of the image into the airy diffraction pattern."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "4a38766a",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "So instead of getting recorded as a single point the light from the point source is spread over several pixels"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "8ecda3e1",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The PSF effect is reduced by first determining the PSF of the telescope and the detector for the particular observation and the measured PSF is used to correct for the observed image through deconvolution "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "041af45b",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## NIRCam Detector and non-destructive readout"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "818dd241",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "<img src="integration.jpg" width="700">"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "f350938b",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "JWST NIRCam employs a non-destructive readout technique. When photons from an astronomical source enters the detector pixel, they generate electrons and the accumulated charge increases linearly with time.Unlike a conventional detector readout where the charges are removed after being measured, the NIRCam detector can be read repeatedly without destroying the charge making the same pixel be measured several times during a single exposure."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "8190147e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### Frame"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "882c5c05",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "A Frame is one complete readout of the NIRCam detector. During one frame the detector measures the amount of charge that has been gathered in each pixel at that moment.The pixel can be read repeatedly as exposure continues."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "cfd00dc0",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The blue marks represents the frame data,the gray ones marks the dropped frames. It is a detector read that is not retained due to reset of readout timing to let the electronics settle, so that it can maintain the requried timing."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "3ac7bc5f",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### Groups"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "88abed84",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The collection of consecutive detector frames are together known as a group.The detector continues accumulating charge from one group to the next, so successive groups represent progressively later measurements of the signal. For example, the first group gives an early measurement of the accumulated signal, while the second and third groups give measurements after more charge has accumulated. Thus, the groups provide the sequence of measurements needed to describe how the detector signal changes with time."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "5271c590",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "For NIRCam there are 8 frames comprising of one group so we can say \n",
    "```text\n",
    "NFRAMES=8\n",
    "```\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "b2ff23e4",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### Integration"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "62f8d62d",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "An integration is one continuous period during which the detector accumulates signal while being read repeatedly. It starts with detector being reset and then proceeds through a sequence of groups until the integration ends. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "e7252b59",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "For NIRCam observation \n",
    "```text \n",
    "NINTS=1\n",
    "```\n",
    "Meaning that there is one integration per exposure for our observation, there can be many intgrations per exposure. Total all the integrations together gives us an exposure"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "8e697b2f",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "NGROUPS=9\n",
    "\n",
    "One integration has 9 groups"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "9c986e56",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Stage 1\n",
    "\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "783a598f",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Data Quality Initialization "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "760578d8",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "When JWST was built they took tons of exposure in various ways like in complete darkness,uniform illumination and different temperature and analysed every pixel on how it behaved. So to identify a dead pixel they illuminated the detector with uniform light source and the good pixels responded but the ones which showed near zero signal were considered dead pixels.\n",
    "\n",
    "Instead of trying to eliminate every defective pixel, astronomers take a different approach. Before launch they spend months carefully measuring the behaviour of every pixel. They determine exactly which pixels are permanently dead, which are unusually noisy, which saturate too early, which have unstable dark current, and which behave normally. This information is stored in the MASK reference file used during the DQ Initialization step."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "174a6036",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "During this step it loads the reference file and fetches the flag values of the pixels"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "2e0c0cff",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "```text\n",
    "GOOD -  0\n",
    "DN_USE -1\n",
    "SATURATED-2\n",
    "JUMP_DET-4\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "7c0ec278",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "These flags are stored in PIXELDQ and GROUPDQ, and the calibration steps updates these arrays when it encounters pixels that needs to be assigned flags as mentioned above"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "648b64c2",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Saturation"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "74032ed2",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Saturation occurs when a detector pixel receives so much signal that it reaches the maximum charge or signal level that it can reliably store and measure. A detector pixel has a finite capacity, called its full-well capacity. As photons enter the detector, they generate electrons, and the number of accumulated electrons increases with time. For a normal, unsaturated pixel, the relationship between accumulated signal and exposure time is approximately linear"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "dc6ac75a",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "For example, a detector pixel that can reliably hold approximately 100000 electrons. If an astronomical source produces 20000 electrons during an exposure the detector can measure the signal normally. If the signal continues to increase to 80000 it is still below saturation but if it reaches more than 100000 it has reached its capacity, any additional photons arriving later cannot be measured normally. It means that that pixel is saturated"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "dc4db489",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Before observation they measure the limit of the reliable signal that every detector can hold, the limit is stored in CRDS. Pipeline process its pixel by pixel and group by group. If a particular pixel in group 3 is flagged it stores this flag in GROUPDQ. During ramp fit it notices this and the groups above this are segmented from below individual slopes are found and their weighted averages are done to have a smooth slope for rate fits."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "aaf4135a",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Superbias Subtraction "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "853daa4b",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "In an ideal detector, if no astronomical light is falling on the detector, the measured signal should be zero. However, real detectors and their readout electronics produce a small signal even when no light is present. This unwanted electronic signal is called the bias level or bias signal. It is essentially an electronic offset introduced by the detector readout system.\n",
    "\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "986c466a",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "To get the bias the CCD image has an exposure time of zero seconds. The shutter remains closed and the CCD is simply read out. The purpose of a bias or zero frame is to allow the user to determine the underlying noise level within each data frame.\n",
    "Small electrical offset reference file is created by pointing the camera to a black field where astronomical background is extremely faint "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "211fd822",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "For JWST, the concept is slightly different from the simple CCD bias-frame picture. JWST's infrared detectors use non-destructive multiple reads, and the detector data are organized into frames, groups, and integrations. The calibration therefore uses a superbias reference file rather than simply subtracting one ordinary CCD bias frame.\n",
    "This reference file contains a 2D map which is supplied to the JWST calibration pipeline through the Calibration Reference Data System (CRDS).  And this offset is subtracted from every group."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "8ba50f7a",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "\n",
    "The superbias reference file is created through a combination of both, ground testing before launch and in-flight calibration measurements. The detector is read immediately after being reset with essentially no time for photons or dark current to accumulate. What you measure in this state is purely the electronic reset level of each pixel.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "d7c42328",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Refferal Pixel Correction"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "32d83d80",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The signal in any readout would differ from that in pprevious readout due to photons as well as readout electronics.Amplifiers add a constant value to each pixel and this differs from amplifier in one group to the other.offset produced throughtout the exposure is not constant.\n",
    "Along with amplifier nosie there is electronic noise that changes gradually across the detector rows.Instead of every row receiving  the same offset one row might get more or less."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "35a5465f",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Each row of pixels is connected to a shared horizontal output line called a row bus.The horizontal scan is fast because it just switches transistors along the already-connected row bus one by one. The fast axis is the direction the readout moves rapidly horizontally across each row. The slow axis is the direction it moves slowly vertically from row to row.\n",
    "The amplifier reads pixel voltages and converts them to numbers. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "55697e12",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "This produces a horizontal striping which is known as 1/f noise. The 1/f noise appears as stripes along the slow axis direction because the amplifier drift is slow compared to the fast horizontal scan but fast compared to the time to read the whole detector."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "a9437615",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Calibration pipeline needs an independent way to measure electronic noise, so they have introduced reference pixels which does not have a photosensitive layer so it doesn’t detect light but the readout electronics are connected so signal measured is only the detector electronics.\n",
    "\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "7d0562d8",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "For NIRCAM reference pixels are the 4 pixel wide border around the 4 edges: Odd, even, top and bottom.\n",
    "NIRCam detectors the odd-numbered columns and the even-numbered columns behave slightly differently because they are read through slightly different electronic pathways. The pipeline calculates the reference signal separately for odd columns and even columns instead of treating all columns the same.\n",
    "The top and bottom reference pixels mainly tell the pipeline about an overall electronic offset, while the side reference pixels reveal how the electronic drift changes from one detector row to another."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "860f3d8f",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Linearity Correction"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "1ce02e81",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "As the light increases every group the light should follow the signal linearly doubt the light doubt the signal. One digital number is equal to two electrons, so two DN should be 4 electrons but this works well for empty detectors as pixels easily collects electrons so it is fully linear. But as the pixel’s capacity is almost full the field in the pixels become weaker potential is low for the electrons to remain inside the potential efficiency shrinks and it becomes harder for the newly created electrons to remain inside the potential, which inturn reduces the collecting efficiency, so it needs a correction.\n",
    "\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "81139078",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Before launch illuminate the detector with uniform light collect plot and obtain a curve fitting of nth order polynomial the co efficient are stored in reference files. Correction is represented by nth order polynomial for each pixel in the detector with n + 1 array of co-efficient from the reference file."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "0f14735e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Dark Subtraction"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "3957617b",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Dark current is the electrical signal produced by a detector even when no astronomical light is falling on it. In an ideal detector, if there were no incoming photons, the measured signal would remain zero apart from electronic offsets and noise. In a real semiconductor detector, however, thermal energy can generate charge carriers inside the detector material. Thermally generated electrons contribute to the measured signal even when the detector is completely dark. This unwanted signal is called dark current. Therefore dark current is an instrumental background that must be measured and removed during calibration."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "7bbaa948",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The DARK reference file contains pixel-by-pixel and frame-by-frame dark current values for a given detector readout mode."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c50f3865",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Jump Detection"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "6c560b1c",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Cosmic rays deposit large amount of energy into pixels instantaneously, which leads to sudden abnormal changes in the signal recorded by the detector. For a pixel receiving constant amount of light the measured signal should increase approximately smoothly and linearly with time. But the sudden increase between two consecutive reads says that there is large influx of photons."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "08eb2a1c",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "For example imagine the measured signal from one ppixel to another is smooth increase from 100,200,300 digital numbers. But now at the fourth pixel if the value is read as 1200, there is a sudden increase. This sudden increase is called jump. The signal after the jump may continue increasing normally."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "7e210c06",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The detector flags such jumps in JUMP_DET array and also divides the reads into two segments one before the jump which is a smooth linear increase and second for the saturated pixels. The second segment is terminated. Once the useful segments are identified they are fitted using the least square fit."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "a878e789",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Ramp(Slope) Fitting"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "462559bb",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Ramp fitting converts multiple non-destructive detector reads obtainted during an integration into a single count rate measurement for each pixel. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "590b4b9a",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "JWST infrared detector is read repeatedly without destroying the charge that has been accumulated.It is called up the ramp The repeated measurement helps determine how quickly the signal is building up with time. The rate of which is calculated by Rampfit step."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "1431acf1",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The data entering RampFit have already passed through the earlier detector-level calibration steps, and problematic measurements have been identified using the Data Quality (DQ) flags. For example, if a pixel becomes saturated, the saturated measurement is flagged; if a cosmic ray suddenly deposits additional charge, the Jump Detection Step identifies the sudden discontinuity and flags the affected data. These flagged measurements are then rejected or appropriately treated during the ramp fit so that they do not produce an incorrect count rate.Then it fits the remaining up-the-ramp data with a straight line using ordinary least squares, and uses the slope of that line to determine the pixel's mean count rate in counts per second."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "340116c0",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The rate.fits file is the output produced after ramp fitting in Stage 1. It contains a 2D count-rate image, where each pixel represents the measured count rate in counts per second obtained from the up-the-ramp data."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "ad478e97",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Implementation of Detector1 Pipeline: JWST Pipeline "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "9d989c7a",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "For the data reduction and calibration of the JWST NIRCam observations, I use the JWST Calibration Pipeline Notebooks repository maintained by the Space Telescope Science Institute (STScI). The repository is hosted on GitHub under the spacetelescope organization and contains Python-based Jupyter notebooks designed to demonstrate how JWST observations can be processed using the official jwst science calibration pipeline,which is accessed via \"https://github.com/spacetelescope/jwst-pipeline-notebooks/blob/main/notebooks/NIRCAM/Imaging/JWPipeNB-nircam-imaging.ipynb\""
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "6e1bb651",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "This notebook provides a framework to process the NIRCam Imaging data, which are divided in three stages pf pipeline.After downloading the raw level 1b NIRCam data as explained in \"[Notebook1](Report_Notebook1.ipynb)\". The next steps are to process the detector level data using JWST Detector1Pipeline also known as 'calwebb_detector1\".The overall purpose of Stage 1 is therefore to take the raw detector measurements and transform them into scientifically useful calibrated count-rate data that can be passed to the subsequent Stage 2 imaging calibration."
   ]
  },
